    "plasticShrinkageRef": "NRMCA Concrete in Practice #5: Plastic Shrinkage Cracking",
    "dryingShrinkageDesc": "The most common cause of concrete cracking. Because almost all concrete is mixed with more water than is needed to hydrate the cement, much of the remaining water evaporates, causing the concrete to shrink.",
    "dryingShrinkageRef": "NRMCA Concrete in Practice #4: Cracking Concrete Surfaces",
    "recommendedTitle": "Dolese Bros. Co. Recommended Practices",
    "recommendedIntro": "To reduce the effects of adverse weather on concrete:",
    "practice1": "Pour during cooler temperatures, such as early morning or at night.",
    "practice2": "Don't get too spread out - Have enough manpower to quickly place, finish and cure the concrete.",
    "practice3": "Moisten the subgrade and form work prior to concrete placement.",
    "practice4": "Limit the effect of wind and sun by using windbreaks and sunshades.",
    "practice5": "Use a superplasticizer, if needed, for a concrete consistency that allows rapid placement. Try not to add water - more water = more potential for cracking.",
    "practice6": "Prevent loss of surface moisture from the plastic concrete through use of evaporation retarders.",
    "practice7": "Ask our Dispatch Department or your local Dolese Bros. Co. Sales Representative about using synthetic fibers to help control plastic shrinkage cracks.",
    "practice8": "Provide the recommended curing methods as soon as possible after the concrete finishing.",
